**Proof.** The ordered simplex has the eight stated vertices: for any
ordered $x$, its barycentric weights are
$1-x_1,x_1-x_2,\ldots,x_6-x_7,x_7$. They are nonnegative and sum to one.
Let $\bar v=156^{-1}\sum_{v\text{ in the vertex table}}v$. Direct substitution
in the primitive halfspaces gives $b_h-A_h\bar v>0$ for every $h$, with
minimum $1/156$. More explicitly its coordinates are

$$
\bar v=\left(\frac{9687993987232052437621499636453}{13387599618260092496810385054208},\frac{350429675989364569915572681628814707}{541992406590844528792744526288342400},\frac{346955365690705310115619191075684307}{541992406590844528792744526288342400},\frac{295886441907707400608904980138791427}{526051453455819689710604981397508800},\frac{640571247808826290898711002124217457}{1277553529821276389297183526251092800},\frac{1333425824190477777069134832100263971}{3577149883499573890032113873503059840},\frac{952693090058610660404623733006141719}{3577149883499573890032113873503059840}\right).
\tag{158.2}
$$

This proves that every intermediate intersection is full-dimensional,
independently of whether the proposed final table is exhaustive.

For two distinct old vertices $u,v$, their midpoint has exactly the
inequalities active at both endpoints. Its minimal face therefore has
dimension $7-\operatorname{rank}\{A_h:h\text{ active at }u\text{ and }v\}$.
They are endpoints of an edge exactly when that rank is six. Upon
intersection with one halfspace, a new vertex that was not an old vertex
lies in the relative interior of an old face. One added equation makes
that face zero-dimensional only if the old face is one-dimensional.
Thus every new vertex lies on a crossing old edge and is given by
(158.1); conversely every such crossing is a new vertex. Old vertices
with nonnegative new slack are retained, duplicates being identified.

Applying this rank criterion to all old vertex pairs gives exactly the
crossing pairs in Definition 158.1. Substitution in (158.1), retention,
and lexicographic ordering give the next row's old set. This is an
induction from the complete eight-vertex simplex through all 67 rows;
it proves the exhaustive final table, rather than merely verifying a
collection of feasible points. For specificity, the successive old sets
contain altogether 576961 unordered vertex pairs and 38559 edges.
Among those pairs, 6744 distinct common-active sets contain at least
six normals. There are 1171 positive/negative pairs with at least six
common-active normals requiring rank determination. The actual ranks, not merely the number of common equations,
are used in the criterion. Each final vertex has active-normal rank seven.

For each row take the rank of the differences of its tight vertices.
These ranks and tight-vertex counts are the displayed face table.
Exactly 59 rows have rank six, and their primitive supporting hyperplanes
are distinct. Every facet of a full-dimensional halfspace intersection
is supported by a defining row, so the list is complete. Rows 62--69
are eight distinct six-dimensional mixed-head facets. The remaining
rows do not become additional facets. Boundedness follows already
from the ordered-simplex rows. $\square$

